WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.889
- I've never, ever been
motivated by money.

00:00:06.889 --> 00:00:08.301
- It's not about money for me.

00:00:08.301 --> 00:00:11.172
- The goal really was not so
much to make a lot of money.

00:00:11.172 --> 00:00:13.862
- We created the company
not because I wanted to be

00:00:13.862 --> 00:00:16.468
some successful entrepreneur
and make a bunch of money.

00:00:16.468 --> 00:00:17.386
- They didn't make money.

00:00:17.386 --> 00:00:18.979
- You have to be willing
to not make money at it.

00:00:18.979 --> 00:00:21.277
- Sherry and I never talked
about how much money we made

00:00:21.277 --> 00:00:23.042
or how much money we wanted to make.

00:00:23.042 --> 00:00:24.991
- Nothing I did where the only reason

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:27.975
for doing it was the
money was ever worth it.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:31.021
- Business doesn't have to
be always money-centered.

00:00:31.021 --> 00:00:31.854
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:31.854 --> 00:00:34.650
Today we're talking about
how you shouldn't do it

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.487
for the money, and as always guys,

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:38.458
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:38.458 --> 00:00:40.406
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:42.296
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:42.296 --> 00:00:44.194
and when you write it down yourself,

00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:47.155
it's much more likely to
stick with you as well.

00:00:47.155 --> 00:00:48.491
Enjoy.

00:00:48.491 --> 00:00:51.158
(uptempo music)

00:00:59.942 --> 00:01:02.625
- You make more money than
any actor in the world.

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:03.961
- This too shall pass.

00:01:03.961 --> 00:01:07.844
By the way, I don't even
know if that's true.

00:01:07.844 --> 00:01:11.060
I can tell you I pay more taxes
than anybody I've ever met.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.480
Truth be told, I've never,
ever been motivated by money.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.113
- I got to give it to you, man,

00:01:17.113 --> 00:01:19.815
because you just seem
like such a normal guy.

00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:22.134
- That is my super power, talk to my wife.

00:01:22.134 --> 00:01:23.521
- Exactly, that's what it is.

00:01:23.521 --> 00:01:25.013
Make people believe you're a normal dude.

00:01:25.013 --> 00:01:25.905
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:01:25.905 --> 00:01:29.322
- For me, I never did anything for money,

00:01:30.241 --> 00:01:33.182
right, so it was never about money.

00:01:33.182 --> 00:01:35.901
My experience has been
when people do things

00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:38.585
for money, you make bad choices.

00:01:38.585 --> 00:01:40.506
Find what you love and then you'll learn

00:01:40.506 --> 00:01:43.966
how to make money doing what you love.

00:01:43.966 --> 00:01:47.709
When I changed careers, I was
never changin' to something

00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:52.666
for money, I was changin' to
something I loved more, right,

00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:56.620
and to me, that's really the
only way to keep the passion.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.399
If you have two choices,
one is playing a piano,

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:02.566
and another one is, you know, bowling,

00:02:03.862 --> 00:02:06.564
and you can make more money bowling,

00:02:06.564 --> 00:02:08.258
but you love playing the piano more,

00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:09.793
you got to play the piano.

00:02:09.793 --> 00:02:11.049
It's like you'll tear yourself apart

00:02:11.049 --> 00:02:14.726
if you're not doing the thing
that you love most in life,

00:02:14.726 --> 00:02:16.328
and you know what it is right now.

00:02:16.328 --> 00:02:17.972
Right now there's something

00:02:17.972 --> 00:02:20.721
that you love more than everything.

00:02:20.721 --> 00:02:22.492
Whatever it is that you love crazy

00:02:22.492 --> 00:02:24.506
has to be the thing that
you dedicate your life.

00:02:24.506 --> 00:02:26.579
- Are you going to get paid
upfront for this from Lloyd?

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:27.668
Is Lloyd ever going to cut you a check?

00:02:27.668 --> 00:02:28.725
- Am I getting paid upfront for this?

00:02:28.725 --> 00:02:30.714
- You didn't get your check?

00:02:30.714 --> 00:02:33.837
- I don't know, but it's not about,

00:02:33.837 --> 00:02:36.146
it's not about money for me.

00:02:36.146 --> 00:02:37.109
Like, it's not.

00:02:37.109 --> 00:02:40.265
I wanted to do, I wanted my own network.

00:02:40.265 --> 00:02:42.900
I wanted to put, I wanted
to do something cool,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.493
and it's always about
just me doing something

00:02:45.493 --> 00:02:48.178
that I think is awesome,
and the money will come.

00:02:48.178 --> 00:02:49.620
I'm not, I'm not worried about that.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.875
That'll come when the universe
decides for it to come.

00:02:53.875 --> 00:02:56.391
- Our goal was to get, we
started with four employees,

00:02:56.391 --> 00:02:59.927
and our goal was to get to 50 employees,

00:02:59.927 --> 00:03:03.059
and the goal really was not so
much to make a lot of money.

00:03:03.059 --> 00:03:05.801
I wanted, to me a lot
of money in those days

00:03:05.801 --> 00:03:08.502
was I want to own a house,
I want to have a nice car,

00:03:08.502 --> 00:03:12.807
and I wanted to work
with interesting people

00:03:12.807 --> 00:03:13.892
and do interesting things,

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:15.556
and I thought if I could
control my own environment,

00:03:15.556 --> 00:03:16.716
have a small consulting company,

00:03:16.716 --> 00:03:19.487
and Oracle started out as a
software consulting company.

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:20.749
We worked for Tandem Computer,

00:03:20.749 --> 00:03:22.084
if anyone remembers those guys.

00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:23.447
We worked for IBM.

00:03:23.447 --> 00:03:26.709
We worked for a lot of
different tech companies

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:29.499
in Silicon Valley doing
contract programming,

00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:30.887
fixed price contract programming,

00:03:30.887 --> 00:03:33.155
and we did pretty well, actually,

00:03:33.155 --> 00:03:35.100
and again, the idea was just to have

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.827
a small software contract,
software company,

00:03:37.827 --> 00:03:41.597
bunch of, you know, we thought
were hot shot programmers

00:03:41.597 --> 00:03:43.177
and we thought we could
make a decent living

00:03:43.177 --> 00:03:46.655
doing just that, and quite by
accident, we built a product.

00:03:46.655 --> 00:03:49.245
- Airbnb, we created the company

00:03:49.245 --> 00:03:52.138
not because I wanted to be
some successful entrepreneur

00:03:52.138 --> 00:03:53.530
and make a bunch of money.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.832
If I was going to do that, I
would not have started air beds

00:03:55.832 --> 00:03:57.573
for conferences around the country.

00:03:57.573 --> 00:03:59.033
That was not going to be a good idea.

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:01.033
In fact, I had a mentor,

00:04:01.869 --> 00:04:03.287
I told him about the idea
of Air Bed and Breakfast,

00:04:03.287 --> 00:04:04.297
and he told me, "I hope that's not

00:04:04.297 --> 00:04:05.870
the only idea you're working on."

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.071
And my mom growing up,
all she ever wanted,

00:04:08.071 --> 00:04:08.904
'cause I went to art school.

00:04:08.904 --> 00:04:09.783
She said, "Just make sure you get a job

00:04:09.783 --> 00:04:10.971
that has health insurance,"

00:04:10.971 --> 00:04:13.147
and so when we created Air
Bed and Breakfast, she said,

00:04:13.147 --> 00:04:14.706
"So I guess you never got that
job with health insurance.

00:04:14.706 --> 00:04:17.221
You start having to let
strangers in your homes."

00:04:17.221 --> 00:04:21.129
- I come from a country
where you didn't make money

00:04:21.129 --> 00:04:24.494
because you made movies, you
lost money if you made movies,

00:04:24.494 --> 00:04:28.231
and all of my life, when
I approach a new project,

00:04:28.231 --> 00:04:31.672
and I'm 50 years old and I've done now,

00:04:31.672 --> 00:04:33.435
I don't know how many movies
as producer, director,

00:04:33.435 --> 00:04:37.602
and I still have the same
emotion when I go into a project.

00:04:38.726 --> 00:04:42.893
I go, "How much am I willing
to lose to do that image?"

00:04:43.737 --> 00:04:46.884
And there's always one or
two images in every project

00:04:46.884 --> 00:04:50.449
that you would mortgage
your house, sell your car,

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:53.052
give everything you have to make sure

00:04:53.052 --> 00:04:55.135
that image comes to safe.

00:04:56.498 --> 00:04:58.886
- Yeah, I've heard you talk
about like, Pacific Rim.

00:04:58.886 --> 00:05:00.261
It was the girl with the red shoes.

00:05:00.261 --> 00:05:01.094
- Absolutely.

00:05:01.094 --> 00:05:02.828
- You like, had to make
that movie just to see that.

00:05:02.828 --> 00:05:06.161
- It doesn't matter what you go through,

00:05:07.038 --> 00:05:11.586
it doesn't matter if they
give you X amount of time,

00:05:11.586 --> 00:05:15.044
you need to always protect
the images that are crucial.

00:05:15.044 --> 00:05:16.307
- So, you've sort of arrived

00:05:16.307 --> 00:05:18.816
at these different approaches to writing.

00:05:18.816 --> 00:05:21.114
What was the first, to your mind,

00:05:21.114 --> 00:05:22.926
like, the first writing piece that you did

00:05:22.926 --> 00:05:24.682
that kind of put you on the map?

00:05:24.682 --> 00:05:25.985
Where, not like, "I've arrived,"

00:05:25.985 --> 00:05:27.384
but you're like, "Okay, I actually,

00:05:27.384 --> 00:05:29.487
I actually could make this work,

00:05:29.487 --> 00:05:31.223
or I could actually
really be good at this,"

00:05:31.223 --> 00:05:33.787
or, "Wow, I just got
recognition for this piece."

00:05:33.787 --> 00:05:35.869
I mean, were there any
sort of defining moments

00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:37.515
in your career as a writer?

00:05:37.515 --> 00:05:39.170
- Yeah, I think, I mean, I don't know.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:40.678
Yeah, I mean the things that I remember,

00:05:40.678 --> 00:05:42.796
I remember like I was like, by the way,

00:05:42.796 --> 00:05:44.945
the best way to get started in anything

00:05:44.945 --> 00:05:46.467
is to be willing to work for free.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:49.757
Internships like to me
are the path to anything,

00:05:49.757 --> 00:05:51.904
and I mean, I really think being,

00:05:51.904 --> 00:05:53.966
there's definitely a lot
of people who are entitled.

00:05:53.966 --> 00:05:55.872
Basically, I have so much to say,

00:05:55.872 --> 00:05:58.840
but anyway, if you want
to live in your passion,

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.059
whatever your passion is,

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:02.209
you have to be willing
to not make money at it.

00:06:02.209 --> 00:06:05.116
If you want to do what you
love, you have a choice.

00:06:05.116 --> 00:06:07.374
You can choose money or you
can choose what you love.

00:06:07.374 --> 00:06:09.399
Joseph Campbell has a great
quote that I always use.

00:06:09.399 --> 00:06:10.326
I don't know if you guys know this.

00:06:10.326 --> 00:06:13.527
- Joseph Campbell, so the
hero with a thousand faces.

00:06:13.527 --> 00:06:15.338
- Yeah, the great kind of
professor of mythology,

00:06:15.338 --> 00:06:18.276
but he said the insecure,
have you guys heard this?

00:06:18.276 --> 00:06:20.759
The insecure way is the secure way.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:21.592
I don't know if you guys
have heard that quote,

00:06:21.592 --> 00:06:24.057
and what it means basically is, you know,

00:06:24.057 --> 00:06:26.508
my parents, and I'm sure
like most people's parents,

00:06:26.508 --> 00:06:28.613
say, "Get a good job,
try and make some money,

00:06:28.613 --> 00:06:30.425
make a good living," 'cause they think

00:06:30.425 --> 00:06:31.789
that's secure if you go and make money,

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:33.813
but if you do a job, you make
money, you lose that job,

00:06:33.813 --> 00:06:36.007
then you lose the money, you have nothing.

00:06:36.007 --> 00:06:36.840
- Right, right, right.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.425
- But he says if you choose your passion,

00:06:38.425 --> 00:06:40.015
it actually doesn't matter
whether you make money

00:06:40.015 --> 00:06:41.694
or lose money because you're
always going to have your passion

00:06:41.694 --> 00:06:43.440
to be happy so it doesn't actually matter.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.273
- And that's your safety net.

00:06:44.273 --> 00:06:46.190
- Right, but the challenge of that

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.817
is that it might be
eight years or four years

00:06:49.817 --> 00:06:51.876
before you're actually
making a living at it,

00:06:51.876 --> 00:06:54.200
versus something else.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:55.843
- Actually what it finally comes down to

00:06:55.843 --> 00:06:59.518
in your business and how you run it

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:01.768
is you first and me second.

00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:06.843
Take that home with you,
you first and me second.

00:07:06.843 --> 00:07:08.320
The first thing you want to do

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:11.801
is help your people succeed
and then you succeed.

00:07:11.801 --> 00:07:13.757
Just keep it in the right order.

00:07:13.757 --> 00:07:14.867
It's not you succeeding

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:18.211
and then hopefully they come out okay.

00:07:18.211 --> 00:07:20.471
It's all about them, not about you,

00:07:20.471 --> 00:07:24.433
and I think that was the
foundation of our business.

00:07:24.433 --> 00:07:27.779
You know, we never, Sherry
and I never talked about

00:07:27.779 --> 00:07:31.150
how much money we made or how
much money we wanted to make.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.274
We talked about how big we'd
like to have our business,

00:07:33.274 --> 00:07:36.587
but it was never a
personal financial goal,

00:07:36.587 --> 00:07:40.877
but our focus was on how
we could enrich the lives

00:07:40.877 --> 00:07:44.133
of our people and make them better,

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:47.803
oftentimes influencing them
with our faith, as well.

00:07:47.803 --> 00:07:49.702
- When you start out, you have to deal

00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:52.500
with the problems of failure.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.656
You need to be thick skinned to learn

00:07:54.656 --> 00:07:57.283
that not every project will survive.

00:07:57.283 --> 00:07:59.809
A freelance life or life in
the arts is sometimes like

00:07:59.809 --> 00:08:03.082
putting messages in
bottles on a desert island

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:06.582
and hoping that someone will
find one of your bottles

00:08:06.582 --> 00:08:09.578
and open it and read it and
put something in a bottle

00:08:09.578 --> 00:08:12.362
that will wash its way back to you.

00:08:12.362 --> 00:08:15.854
Appreciation or a
commission or money or love,

00:08:15.854 --> 00:08:18.475
and you have to accept that
you may put out hundreds

00:08:18.475 --> 00:08:22.944
of things for every bottle
that winds up coming back.

00:08:22.944 --> 00:08:25.799
The problems of failure are problems

00:08:25.799 --> 00:08:28.950
of discouragement, of
hopelessness, of hunger.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.045
You want everything to happen

00:08:30.045 --> 00:08:32.634
and you want it now and things go wrong.

00:08:32.634 --> 00:08:35.012
My first book, a piece of journalism

00:08:35.012 --> 00:08:37.247
I'd done only for the money,

00:08:37.247 --> 00:08:39.509
and which had already bought
me an electric typewriter

00:08:39.509 --> 00:08:44.023
from the advance should've
been a bestseller.

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:45.695
It should've paid me a lot of money.

00:08:45.695 --> 00:08:48.027
If the publisher hadn't gone
into involuntary liquidation

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:50.103
between the first print run selling out

00:08:50.103 --> 00:08:52.965
and the second print run never happening

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:57.034
and before any royalties could
be paid, it would've done,

00:08:57.034 --> 00:09:00.928
and I shrugged, and I still
have my electric typewriter,

00:09:00.928 --> 00:09:02.759
and enough money to pay the
rent for a couple of months

00:09:02.759 --> 00:09:05.365
and I decided that I'd
do my best in the future

00:09:05.365 --> 00:09:08.532
not to write books just for the money.

00:09:09.393 --> 00:09:11.271
If you didn't get the money,
then you didn't have anything,

00:09:11.271 --> 00:09:14.668
and if did work I was proud
of and I didn't get the money,

00:09:14.668 --> 00:09:18.218
well, at least I'd have the work.

00:09:18.218 --> 00:09:21.428
Every now and then, I forget
that rule, and whenever I do,

00:09:21.428 --> 00:09:24.928
the universe kicks me hard and reminds me.

00:09:25.908 --> 00:09:29.115
I don't know that it's an
issue for anybody but me,

00:09:29.115 --> 00:09:31.668
but it's true that nothing
I did where the only reason

00:09:31.668 --> 00:09:34.829
for doing it was the
money was ever worth it

00:09:34.829 --> 00:09:37.162
except as bitter experience.

00:09:38.551 --> 00:09:40.332
Usually, I didn't wind up
getting the money, either.

00:09:40.332 --> 00:09:42.869
(audience laughing)

00:09:42.869 --> 00:09:44.800
The things I did because I was excited

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.044
and wanted to see them exist in reality

00:09:47.044 --> 00:09:48.740
have never let me down
and I've never regretted

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.812
the time I spent on any of them.

00:09:51.812 --> 00:09:53.482
- Everything that we do
in the business world

00:09:53.482 --> 00:09:54.900
is focused on making money.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.295
That's the indicator
that you try to assess,

00:09:58.295 --> 00:10:00.088
how successful we are.

00:10:00.088 --> 00:10:01.869
In the business world,
there's nothing else,

00:10:01.869 --> 00:10:05.800
so that kind of squeezed out
all kinds of social orientation

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.141
of human life and human activity,

00:10:08.141 --> 00:10:10.443
and that's not a turnable situation,

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:11.953
and that unturnable situation

00:10:11.953 --> 00:10:13.885
has created better structure that we have.

00:10:13.885 --> 00:10:15.517
I said business doesn't have to be

00:10:15.517 --> 00:10:18.565
always money-centered,
always self-centered,

00:10:18.565 --> 00:10:22.046
always, or many times,
obsessed with money,

00:10:22.046 --> 00:10:25.483
because human beings, they
are not always about money.

00:10:25.483 --> 00:10:28.446
Human beings are much bigger
than just money making machine.

00:10:28.446 --> 00:10:29.601
We are not robots.

00:10:29.601 --> 00:10:30.801
We are human beings.

00:10:30.801 --> 00:10:32.851
We take care of ourselves individually.

00:10:32.851 --> 00:10:35.477
At the same time, we take
care of everybody else.

00:10:35.477 --> 00:10:37.265
We take care of the planet itself,

00:10:37.265 --> 00:10:40.308
and business world doesn't
have that orientation,

00:10:40.308 --> 00:10:42.615
so at the moment, we can do that.

00:10:42.615 --> 00:10:43.969
We can create a different kind of business

00:10:43.969 --> 00:10:45.823
which is not money-centered.

00:10:45.823 --> 00:10:47.185
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:47.185 --> 00:10:48.431
I hope you enjoyed.

00:10:48.431 --> 00:10:50.075
I'd love to know, what
was your favorite clip?

00:10:50.075 --> 00:10:51.942
What resonated the most with you?

00:10:51.942 --> 00:10:53.121
What are you going to take from this video

00:10:53.121 --> 00:10:56.675
and immediately apply to your
life or your business somehow?

00:10:56.675 --> 00:10:58.333
Please leave it down in the comments.

00:10:58.333 --> 00:10:59.550
I'm super curious to find out.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:00.666
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:11:00.666 --> 00:11:02.005
I believe in you.

00:11:02.005 --> 00:11:04.340
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:08.507
and whatever your one word is,
